;; manifest/chain-deployments.edn — the consensus/sequencer deployments this
;; workspace runs, and how to ask each one whether it is still moving.
;;
;; ## Why this file exists
;;
;; Measured 2026-09-10: `torihiki-validator-v3` — four witnesses, chained
;; HotStuff, `inga.replica`, quorum 3 of 4 — had been halted since 2026-08-15,
;; **26 days**, split 2-2 at height 5616. Two independent measurements agreed:
;; the highest checkpoint every witness holds (5600) was written 2026-08-15
;; 08:03:25Z, and the pacemaker's own view counter (1,496,323, advancing
;; 0.69/s while the height advanced 0) divided out to 25.1 days of spinning.
;;
;; It was noticed once, and that changed nothing — which is worse than not
;; being noticed, and is the actual lesson. On 2026-08-31, sixteen days in,
;; somebody diagnosed it exactly: `inga.replica/sync-ask-from`'s docstring
;; names THIS chain and THIS height, 5616, and the fix landed on inga's main
;; the same day. It never reached the deployment, because `torihiki-node`'s
;; pin did not move. The chain ran halted for ten more days with its own fix
;; already written.
;;
;; Nothing carried that forward. There was no launchd job, no observatory
;; entry, and the repo bot for `torihiki-node` measures `checkout / pinned /
;; landed / readme / test-signal` — five floors, none of which asks whether
;; the chain moves. The repository was green the whole time. A diagnosis in a
;; docstring is something somebody knew once; a detector is something the
;; workspace keeps knowing.
;;
;; That is the shape CLAUDE.md's eight questions describe: a check that could
;; not see the failure returns the value of a check that looked and found
;; nothing. So the answer is not another repo floor. It is a detector that
;; asks the deployment itself, which is what `scripts/verify-chain-liveness.cljs`
;; does and what this file tells it to ask.
;;
;; ## Fields
;;
;;   :id          keyword, stable. Findings are keyed under it forever.
;;   :kind        :consensus (a witness set that votes) or :sequencer (one
;;                writer, no votes). A sequencer cannot fork or lose a quorum,
;;                so only the liveness half of the detector applies to it.
;;   :chain-id    what the deployment calls itself. Checked against `/head`:
;;                a reply from a chain with a different id is NOT this
;;                deployment answering, and is reported rather than believed.
;;   :base        origin. `/head` and `/block` hang off it.
;;   :witnesses   the names, in the order the set was defined. Single-element
;;                for a sequencer.
;;   :witness-param  query parameter naming a witness, or nil when the
;;                deployment serves exactly one.
;;   :quorum      how many must agree. Recorded so a finding can say what the
;;                votes it counted were short OF, rather than just counting.
;;   :note        one line: what this deployment is for.
;;
;; **Adding a deployment here does not deploy anything.** It adds a question.
{:deployments
 [{:id :torihiki-validator-v3
   :kind :consensus
   :chain-id "torihiki-engi-devnet-1"
   :base "https://torihiki-validator-v3.04-feasts-minded.workers.dev"
   :witnesses ["w1" "w2" "w3" "w4"]
   :witness-param "w"
   :quorum 3
   :note "torihiki (the exchange state machine) executing blocks ordered by inga, four Durable Object witnesses. Generations v1 and v2 answer `Gone` and are deliberately absent."}

  {:id :torihiki-node
   :kind :sequencer
   :chain-id "torihiki-devnet-1"
   :base "https://torihiki-node.04-feasts-minded.workers.dev"
   :witnesses ["_"]
   :witness-param nil
   :quorum 1
   :note "the single-writer Durable Object sequencer. `/head` says `consensus: none` about itself. This is the deployment that actually carries an order book."}]}
